   741	        /// Process input until the first byte that reaches one of
   742	        /// `boundaries`, and stop right after that byte.
   743	        ///
   744	        /// Returns the stop, or null if the whole input was consumed
   745	        /// without reaching a boundary. Only the consumed bytes are
   746	        /// processed, so handler actions never run for bytes after the
   747	        /// stop. Feed the rest of the input in a later call.
   748	        ///
   749	        /// The bytes consumed across a series of calls are exactly the
   750	        /// bytes the stream parsed, in order, and a second stream fed those
   751	        /// same bytes is in the same parser state at every stop. This is
   752	        /// what a proxy that sits between a program and a terminal needs.
   753	        /// It can find exactly where a control string starts and ends, or
   754	        /// wait for a safe point before inserting its own output, using
   755	        /// the same parse that drives its terminal.
   756	        ///
   757	        /// `.ground` is a state, not an event. If the stream is already at
   758	        /// ground, this consumes nothing and returns a stop with
   759	        /// `consumed == 0`. A loop that keeps asking for `.ground` with the
   760	        /// same input never makes progress. After a ground stop, feed the
   761	        /// next bytes without `.ground` in the set, or with `nextSlice`.
   762	        ///
   763	        /// This loop finds the content of every APC string in `input`.
   764	        /// DCS and OSC strings work the same way with their own
   765	        /// boundaries:
   766	        ///
   767	        /// ```zig
   768	        /// const set: Boundary.Set = .initMany(&.{ .apc_start, .apc_end });
   769	        /// var rest = input;
   770	        /// var content_start: ?usize = null;
   771	        /// while (stream.nextSliceUntil(rest, set)) |stop| {
   772	        ///     const offset = input.len - rest.len;
   773	        ///     if (stop.start != null) content_start = offset + stop.consumed;
   774	        ///     if (stop.end != null) {
   775	        ///         // The ending byte is consumed but is not content.
   776	        ///         const content_end = offset + stop.consumed - 1;
   777	        ///         handleApc(input[content_start.?..content_end]);
   778	        ///     }
   779	        ///     rest = rest[stop.consumed..];
   780	        /// }
   781	        /// ```
   782	        ///
   783	        /// The loop needs no special case for a string that ends with the
   784	        /// two-byte terminator `ESC \`. The stop lands on the ESC, and the
   785	        /// backslash is just the next byte of `rest`.
